\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot {t}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using direct integration we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-2)-(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-1)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{26}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186532704 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{