that there were $64\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}$ teams remaining after $6$ rounds." 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186538935 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186538935 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186538935 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186538935 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186538937 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{12} & =-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{12}) & =(-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ \\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $x$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{x}{12} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{12x}{12} & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}x}{\\cancel{12}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186538937 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{12} & =-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{12}\\right) & =(-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $x$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{x}{12} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{12x}{12} & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}x}{\\cancel{12}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186538944 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=110\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the container, and $R=\\frac{100}{2}=50\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=60\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{110} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5h}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{5h}{11})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi (60)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{90\\,000}{121}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water height is decreasing at a rate of $12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186538944 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=110\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the container, and $R=\\frac{100}{2}=50\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=60\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of