{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186541123 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of minerals enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *minerals* enter the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which minerals leave the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n180+20t-30t=(180-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of minerals in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *minerals* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(18-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|18-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(18-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(18-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(18-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q) & =(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (18-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(18-t)^{-4}Q & =0.8(18-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3}) & =0.8(18-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(18-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(18-t)^{-3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(18-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)+C(18-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of minerals. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ C(18-0)^{3}+0.4(18-0) & =5 \\\\ C(18)^{3}+7.2 & =5 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}\\end{al