\\right)" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544617 1080 "x={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544617 1080 "x={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544782 1074 "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544782 1074 "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544821 1074 "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544821 1074 "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544844 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=arctan\\,(\\frac{12}{5})≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544844 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544850 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fe/fe0207214fb16ac0871e15f3be7c9fa33670a7df3617f5f9e0b92b45a09c76af.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{4})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544850 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fe/fe0207214fb16ac0871e15f3be7c9fa33670a7df3617f5f9e0b92b45a09c76af.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Further,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544858 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx}) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544858 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544870 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{7})=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544870 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544872 1074 "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544872 1074 "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544879 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/95/9596107dc58d6acb67f1014ebc17e632dd03173aa975fa84b196588db873d8e5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})≈0.540\\,42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.540\\,42≈3.68\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544879 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/95/9596107dc58d6acb67f1014ebc17e632dd03173aa975fa84b196588db873d8e5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)≈0.540\\,42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.540\\,42≈3.68\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544882 1074 "Since the customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next ticket is resolved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the ticket resolving time, in minutes. Since the team resolves $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}$ tickets per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{5}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5},$ $s=20,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20+10\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X>10) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/5)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.135.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544882 1074 "Since the customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next ticket is resolved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the ticket resolving time, in minutes. Since the team resolves $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}$ tickets per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5},$ $s=20,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20+10\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X>10) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/5)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.135.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544889 1074 "Since barges pass continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time between two successive barges using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between two successive barges. Since a barge passes every $10$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ barges per minute.\n\nThe time interval between two successive barges can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<7) & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-7/10}+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & ≈0.1099\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544889 1074 "Since barges pass continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time between two successive barges using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between two successive barges. Since a barge passes every $10$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ barges per minute.\n\nThe time interval between two successive barges can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<7) & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-7/10}+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & ≈0.1099\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186544895 1074 "Since the cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next pothole as an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, until the cyclist encounters the next pothole. Since the cyclist encounters, on average, $4$ potholes per hour (or $1$ pothole every $15$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ potholes per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 30+10\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X\\le 10) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.487.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186544895 1074 "Since the cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next pothole as an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, until the cyclist encounters the next pothole. Since the cyclist encounters, on average, $4$ potholes per hour (or $1$ pothole every $15$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ potholes per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 30+10\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X\\le 10) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.487.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186545132 1074 "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{1}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-(- \\frac{7}{2}))^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{1}{2}))^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y+\\frac{1}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+\\frac{1}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{1