74 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical axis, we need to solve the equation of our second curve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-4\\,⟹\\,x=y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7e/7e08a30f9ef1f8ce748ba0fcac73f1e93d994e4fd3a3d3d1367568c89c29f4d0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y+4.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y+4.$\n\n![](images/26/262546bf3f1d1c9025d23b9fb134c9de7b5299c7b6383563531dffa62244163d.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,(y+4)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[y+6]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/ce/cefab13ca3d09fcf596e7906384c2efd1718c289783792f1b0ff950632c823ad.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({y}^{2}+2)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+4]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of our solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[y+6]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+4]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({y}^{2}+12y+36-({y}^{4}+8{y}^{2}+16))\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}(12y+20-{y}^{4