rac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}-\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12})+(\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{7}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12}-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12}-\\frac{1}{16})}{(0.2898)\\cdot (0.2764)} \\\\  & ≈-0.06,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186546989 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{1}{2}xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{3x}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3x}+\\frac{1}{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}+\\frac{3}{8})-(\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}-\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12})+(\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{7}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12}-\\frac{1}{16}.\\