 $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician and integration by parts, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-y)]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-8\\pi +16}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}}{(0.4332)\\cdot (0.4332)} \\\\  & ≈0.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186547013 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician and integration by parts, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-y)]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}-\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-8\\pi +16}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}}{(0.4332)\\cdot (0.4332)} \\\\  & ≈0.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186547018 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nNote that pure water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $14\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of solution; water enters at $14\\,\\text{L/min}$ and drains out at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+14t-10t=(500+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed sugar-water solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+4t}\