194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186554574 1074 "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $2\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186554574 1074 "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $2\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f\\