,$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,$ $n=2,$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nSince $n=2,$ we need to compute the first $2-1=1$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186562447 1074 "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186562447 1074 "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{\\left(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186562462 1074 "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-4<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{1+4}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4(x-1)(x-4) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{5}{2}-1)(\\frac{5}{2}-4) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{5}{2},9),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,9],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 9.$\n\n![](images/70/70dc863f5f089c9434d783495c31b77fd7a0ac84a852d2b32222b445f463efe3.png)" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186562462 1074 "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-4<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{1+4}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4(x-1)(x-4) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{5}{2}-1)(\\frac{5}{2}-4) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{5}{2},9),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,9],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 9.$\n\n![](images/70/70dc863f5f089c9434d783495c31b77fd7a0ac84a852d2b32222b445f463efe3.png)" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186562479 1074 "![](images/e2/e2c83b965f7108bff3165531446cce43006a41b9742914e8fc40c50741230427.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the pos