n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186564495 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186564501 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5{a}_{1}-8{a}_{2}}{3}){t}^{2}+(\\frac{5{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}}{3})t+(\\frac{5{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}}{3}) \\\\ (\\frac{-4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}}{3}){t}^{2}+(\\frac{-4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}}{3})t+(\\f