s\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =2\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{15\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186567013 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={337.5}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is equivalent to ${337.5}^{∘},$ which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/a2/a248eaf8b200e2788db08164f77c2d3d457837e851726f54ca22f19cbc1dc440.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =2\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{15\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186567021 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186567021 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186567024 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$ in terms of a reference angle." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186567024 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186567024 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={132}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is equivalent to ${132}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/23/23a979aa6a346c2572ecc108e3b88ac4cbe3f192dc6818c975c9373cbca33e74.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}).\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186567024 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={132}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is equivalent to ${132}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/23/23a979aa6a346c2572ecc108e3b88ac4cbe3f192dc6818c975c9373cbca33e74.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right).\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186567041 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot (-G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186567041 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot (-G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(-\\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186567042 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y))\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot 1}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)){e}^{x+y}}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y).\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [175921