}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/f4/f4609e6433a861ef255cbeeac60c8a316e5c14a89df45030e26e526e3a1253a4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186570042 1074 "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{