 temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{8}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\frac{10}{3}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3}\\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3}\\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)=-\\frac{10\\times 3}{3\\times 8}=-\\frac{10}{8}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\frac{5}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186570742 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =4-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =4-{sec}^{2}⁡(0) \\\\  & =4-{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-{(\\frac{1}{1})}^{2}=4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $3.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186570742 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =4-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =4-{sec}^{2}⁡(0) \\\\  & =4-{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =4-{\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)}^{2}=4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneou