2 1071 "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186578812 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nLet's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{2}+1.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186578812 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nLet's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordina