} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186578832 1074 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))+(x)(\\frac{1}{x+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186578832 1074 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))+(x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186578833 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{4}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{3})(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1))+({x}^{4})(-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)-3{x}^{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)(4-3x\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(1)^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3(1)-1)(4-3(1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(1)-1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡2(4-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186578833 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{4}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{3})(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1))+({x}^{4})(-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)-3{x}^{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)(4-3x\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)|}_{x=1} & =(1)^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3(1)-1)(4-3(1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(1)-1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡2(4-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186578835 1074 "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-4<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola a