ac{16}{3}(\\frac{{5}^{4-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-\\frac{{5}^{3-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\left(\\frac{5-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186580259 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2{x}^{3}+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2{x}^{3}+4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 6 & 20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+4}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{6}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{20}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186580259 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2{x}^{3}+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2{x}^{3}+4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 6 & 20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+4}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{ali