n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0+0=0.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186580303 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186580303 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186580303 1074 "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we know the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we can draw the general shape of the tangent function, but stretch it so that it fills up the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/189aa1ea4e33d47bd26027af4990020c837211a58d53668e25f9210353faa784.png)" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186580303 1074 "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\