tion if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not*  a surjection.  There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In fact, ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $\\in R.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Z.$ There is no number $x\\in Z$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Z.$\n\n- III is a surjection. Given $y\\in Q,$ choose $x=-\\frac{y}{2}\\in Q.$ Then $h(x)=h(-\\frac{y}{2})=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{2})=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186580945 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not*  a surjection.  There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In fact, ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $\\in R.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Z.$ There is no number $x\\in Z$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Z.$\n\n- III is a surjection. Given $y\\in Q,$ choose $x=-\\frac{y}{2}\\in Q.$ Then $h(x)=h\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)=-2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186580948 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n