ius $r=6\\,\\text{ft}.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186581678 1071 "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186581678 1071 "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186581678 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186581678 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=1.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186581681 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{5}{3{x}^{3}}|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186581681 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{5}{3{x}^{3}}|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true]