ned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)-1 & =2x \\\\ x-5 & =2x \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-5\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-5.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186583584 1074 "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators ${x}^{2}$ and $x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({x}^{2},x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\right)-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\right) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}}\\right) \\\\ (x-4)-1 & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)-1 & =2x \\\\ x-5 & =2x \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-5\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-5.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186583590 1074 "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{