ht)=DNE.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186583651 1074 "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}2y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{y}^{2}]_{x}^{1-x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}((1-x)^{2}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}(1-2x)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2x]_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186583651 1074 "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}2y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{y}^{2}]_{x}^{1-x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}((1-x)^{2}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}(1-2x)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2x]_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186584356 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+9\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+9\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{10})-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186584356 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+9\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-x+9\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{10}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{10}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186584360 1074 "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-9$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x &=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-9)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{7}{6},$ then $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi )=\\frac{7}{6}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi )+2 & =18(\\frac{7}{6})+2 \\\\  & =21+2 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186584360 1074 "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-9$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x &=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-9)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{7}{6},$ then $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi )=\\frac{7}{6}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-18\\pi )+2 & =18\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)+2 \\\\  & =21+2 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186584370 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{6x+1}=3x-1,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6x+1} & =3x-1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{6x+1})}^{2} & =(3x-1)^{2} \\\\ 6x+1 & =9{x}^{2}-6x+1 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ 3x(3x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=0$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6(0)+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3(0)-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 1 & \\ne -1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=0$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=\\frac{4}{3}$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6(\\frac{4}{3})+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3(\\frac{4}{3})-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{8+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=\\frac{4}{3}$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{6x+1}=3x-1$  is $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186584370 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{6x+1}=3x-1,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6x+1} & =3x-1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{6x+1})}^{2} & =(3x-1)^{2} \\\\ 6x+1 & =9{x}^{2}-6x+1 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ 3x(3x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the