2186576411 false] [17592186589981 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $V$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(v)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le v\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $V$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{2},2],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(v)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}, & -\\frac{1}{2}\\le v\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le V<\\frac{1}{2}) & =P(-\\frac{1}{2}\\le V<\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}f(v)\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186589990 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{3}X] & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186589990 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{3}X] & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186589995 1074 "Our goal is to express $V$ in terms of $h.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation, which we do using cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2r & =h \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}\\end{aligned}\