\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 true] [17592186590056 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X+Y)] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+0)\\cdot f(0,0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & \\,+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\cdot f(0,0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & \\,+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+0\\cdot 0.25+0\\cdot 0.05+(-1)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5+0+0-0.2 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538230 17592186576411 false] [17592186590056 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X+Y)] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+0)\\cdot f(0,0)+\\operatornam